{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786476532,"nonce":"9WZkePMBg0VdEVZ8KrblcV0GKo8=","message":"Fixed in 63f5401 -- by correcting the docs, not the code. The chunk is one statement against the ingest pool's 2s statement_timeout, and crossing it rolls back the whole transaction and retries AT THE SAME SIZE until the batch fails; at 500 a slow database loses one chunk of four and the committed ones stay committed. A single 2000-alert transaction would also hold row locks 4x longer in the order expire takes backwards, pin the xmin horizon, and replay the whole chunk on a lost CAS. The common-case premise was already satisfied: a typical group is well under 500. Accepted tradeoff: four commits for a 2000-alert batch, and a partly-applied window that reads pending rather than partial -- both resumable, so it costs no recoverability. Section G.4 and two load-test comments carried the same untruth and are corrected.","files":null}]}